  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1213 PM MDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nThings have warmed up fairly quickly this morning, with multiple \nobservations at or approaching 90F already across the metro area (as \nof 11:30 AM), as an upper-level ridge remains centered over Arizona. \nShould see at least a few more degrees of surface heating before \nhigh-based showers/storms develop bringing some heat relief from \nwest to east across the forecast area. The main threat from \nconvection today remains with gusty outflows/microbursts. While \ninstability is lacking to support very much in terms of strong \nthunderstorms across the majority of the forecast area, better low-\nlevel moisture and instability is expected for our far northeast \ncounties, where we could see an isolated severe storm this evening. \nDespite the lacking instability, DCAPE values are expected to \nrange from 1300-1500 J/kg across the plains, which will be enough \nfor an isolated severe wind gust or two from anything that does \ndevelop. Hi-res guidance keeps this potential mainly for \nnortheastern Larimer County and for areas east of I-25.\nShowers/storms are expected to diminish through the evening, with\nabove normal temperatures expected across the forecast area. \n\nThe next few days will remain fairly active as multiple\ndisturbances move through the flow aloft and the ridge continues\nits eastward trek. For Saturday, the center of the ridge will be\nnear the Texas Panhandle, with flow aloft becoming slightly more \nwest-southwesterly over Colorado. A more pronounced mid-level \ndisturbance and increased PWATs will aid in slightly higher \nshower/storm coverage for the afternoon/evening. Scattered high- \nbased showers/storms are expected to develop by early afternoon, \nwith an isolated severe wind threat once again. Slightly cooler \ntemperatures are expected across the forecast area as 700 mb \ntemperatures see a few degrees of cooling. \n\nFlow aloft will become increasingly southwesterly as the ridge\nmoves eastward through the weekend. Ensembles show PWATs remain\nelevated and we should be able to tap into some monsoonal moisture\nat least at the upper levels, while lower level moisture will\nremain more limited. We should see at least scattered\nshowers/storms each afternoon, with Monday bringing the best\nchances for more meaningful rainfall this week, with increasingly\ndrier conditions expected beyond that. Temperatures are expected\nto see a few days of sub 90 degrees for a change on Monday and \nTuesday across the plains.",
